Marc Schlensog wrote:
> 
> On Sun, 12 Mar 2006 15:10:42 -0500
> uNiX pSyChO <marco@unixpsycho.com> wrote:
> 
> > David J Dachtera wrote:
> >
> > > LK40x KBs were usually used with VT3xx terminals and later
> > > (mini-modular plug). Would he not need an LK45x variant (PS/2)?
> > >
> >
> > oops...  that's what i meant.  LK45x.  :-(
> >
> > worked fine with Digital UNIX but i had a pain finding keymaps for
> > Linux to use those extra keys properly.
> 
> Did you find any? What's the difference between LK45x and LK46x?

LK45x was a PS/2 keyboard 100% compatible with the PCs of the day in
terms of electrical specifications and keycodes.

LK46x is the keyboard usually shipped with an Alpha with a
factory-installed graphics "head". Seems to have slightly different
electrical specifications since connecting a PC PS/2 keyboard to an
Alpha can damage the PC keyboard to the point that a PC might not
recognize it. Seen that here after plugging a Packard-Bell keyboard into
an AlphaStation 200, then trying to use that same keyboard with an ASUS
motherboard in a PC or a Packard-Bell PC. The PC no longer recognizes
the keyboard (it did before I connected to the AlphaStation 200), though
it continues to work with the Alpha.

